## MANDATORY PRE-APPROVAL TEXTBOOK AUDIT

Do not return `approved: true` until you explicitly inspect these four failure modes in every supplied viewport:

1. **Crop-edge audit** — inspect the top, right, bottom, and left edge of every `data-textbook-crop="true"` screenshot. A crop fails when any surrounding instruction, paragraph, caption, heading, answer line, page number, or even a clipped glyph fragment remains visible. Tighten the crop using one recomputed native-image rectangle. Do not infer safety from the HTML classes alone; judge the rendered pixels. The user message includes a decoded `TEXTBOOK GEOMETRY MANIFEST`; use its exact source dimensions and current native rectangle as the starting point instead of guessing from CSS.
2. **Raster/HTML duplicate audit** — compare every readable phrase inside a retained raster with the visible semantic text immediately before and after it. If surrounding prose or an instruction is duplicated, crop it out and keep the semantic leaf visible. If text is intrinsic to the retained visual object (for example a bill label, connected glossary callout, card title, option marker, unit, or price), keep the raster text and return its exact image/text pair in `transcription_updates`; the pipeline adds `sr-only` without changing DOM order. Never move a transcription before its image to imitate print placement. Never approve the same sentence visibly appearing once in the image and once in HTML.
3. **Writable-area audit** — for every activity image with a printed blank, line, cell, or box, count the separate writable regions and verify that each has exactly one visible response affordance. The native control must cover the printed writable area itself, with comparable width and height; it must not sit below, beside, or partly outside it. A still-visible printed blank plus a detached white input is a failure. When correcting coordinates, derive each control's percentages from its native-image `(x, y, width, height)` rectangle and keep those percentages invariant across breakpoints. Use the decoded control rectangles in the geometry manifest, and remove any `min-h-*`/`min-w-*`/`max-h-*`/`max-w-*` class that overrides percentage geometry.
4. **Narrow-viewport audit** — inspect mobile for collisions, clipping, and unreadably small evidence. A number/word/choice bank with many tokens must wrap or use a smaller mobile column count; never approve a fixed one-row grid whose values overlap. Image-anchored controls must remain aligned after the image scales.

When returning corrected HTML, preserve the authoritative `data-id` DOM order. In the reasoning, identify which of these four audits required a change; for any crop or control-coordinate change, state the approximate native-image rectangle used.

When tightening a leaking textbook crop, also return that exact native rectangle in `geometry_updates` as `{ "image_id": "…", "crop": { "x": …, "y": …, "width": …, "height": … } }`. The pipeline applies this structured geometry deterministically, so do not rely only on manually edited Tailwind crop classes. A crop update must be a tighter sub-rectangle of the current decoded crop and must contain no surrounding prose. Return `geometry_updates: []` when no crop changes are needed. If any geometry update is supplied, return `approved: false` so the updated screenshot is reviewed once more.

When a screenshot proves that an intrinsic label is visibly duplicated because the focused plan missed it, return `{ "image_id": "…", "text_id": "…" }` in `transcription_updates`. The text ID must follow that image and precede the next image in the supplied content tree. Do not rewrite or reposition option labels to solve a baked duplicate. Return `transcription_updates: []` when none are needed. If any transcription update is supplied, return `approved: false` so the hidden transcription is re-screenshot and verified.
The update must name the still-visible semantic duplicate itself. For example, if a raster `c)` and semantic `c)` are both visible, use the content-tree ID whose literal text is `c)`, not a nearby title, date, price, or other leaf that is already `sr-only`. Never return an ID that current HTML already marks `sr-only`; that is a no-op and will be rejected.
